WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.041 --> 00:00:05.374
(upbeat marching music)

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.060
- Good morning, Class of
2025, transfer students,

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.400
Eli Whitney students, and
visiting international students.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.383
Welcome to Yale.

00:03:41.528 --> 00:03:44.778
(audience applauding)

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:53.180
I am Marvin Chun, Dean of Yale College.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.370
And joining me on this platform before you

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.920
are the officers of the university,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.725
the heads and deans of
the residential colleges,

00:04:00.725 --> 00:04:02.630
the deans and staff members

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.920
of the Yale College Dean's
office and athletics.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.193
We are so pleased to be
with you today in person.

00:04:11.424 --> 00:04:14.674
(audience applauding)

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:20.950
Family members and friends
who are joining us remotely,

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.100
we extend our warm welcome to you,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.950
and we thank you for
everything you have done

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.630
in guiding these young adults.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.503
Congratulations on this
proud and happy moment.

00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:37.183
(audience applauding)

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.450
Class of 2025, in the
entire 320 year history

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:49.903
of Yale College, you are the
greatest entering class ever.

00:04:51.189 --> 00:04:54.439
(audience applauding)

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.560
In number, with 1,815 of you joining

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.090
as new students this year.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.930
And for the first time, in
at least half a century,

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.430
we are hosting opening
assembly on Cross Campus.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.720
This is a place that you
will come to know well,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.710
because you will be
spending so much time here

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:22.340
in Sterling Memorial Library behind us

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.233
and in Bass Library below us.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:29.660
Surrounding this group are
three of the 14 best residential

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.020
colleges in the world.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.068
Behind you on your left,
Grace Hopper College.

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:38.740
(audience cheering)

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.642
At your front left Trumbull College.

00:05:41.642 --> 00:05:44.210
(audience cheering)

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.853
And flanking the platform,
Berkeley College.

00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:50.800
(audience cheering)

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.320
Where my wife, Professor Woo-kyoung Ahn,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.290
and I raised our two children

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.260
when I served as a head for nine, years.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.963
I literally lived right
there on your right.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.230
And also to your front left,

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.640
there is the Woman's Table
designed by alumni, Maya Lin,

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.080
celebrating what is now 152
years of co-education at Yale

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.760
and 52 years of co-education
in Yale College.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.870
To your far right, a block away,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.129
yes, co-education.

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:22.379
(audience applauding)

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:26.811
To your far right, a short block away,

00:06:26.811 --> 00:06:29.700
is a magnificent Schwartzman Center,

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.653
a newly expanded hub for student life.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:37.080
Our campus more grand than ever
is embedded within the city

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.090
of New Haven, also more vibrant than ever.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.900
And within what is now
the state of Connecticut,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.110
Yale acknowledges that
indigenous peoples and nations,

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:50.260
including Mohegan, Mashantucket
Pequot, Eastern Pequat,

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.210
Schaghticoke, Golden Hill Paugussett,

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.990
Niantic and the Quinnipiac
and other Algonquin

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.430
speaking peoples have
stewarded through generations

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.860
these lands and waterways.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.010
We honor and respect the
enduring relationship

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.933
that exists between these peoples
and nations and this land.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.930
In my role as Dean, I have
the privilege of supporting

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.880
President Salovey's mission for Yale to be

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:17.310
the research university

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.050
most committed to teaching and learning.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.320
What that means is that
I am here to make sure

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.790
that you get the best
liberal arts education

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.030
and extracurricular life
that Yale has to offer

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.273
in the classroom and outside of it.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.150
A good part of what you
will learn here will come

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.972
from the faculty who will teach
you in the classes you take

00:07:35.972 --> 00:07:38.770
while much will come from your peers

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:40.193
and colleagues around you.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.790
Taken together, these teachers
of yours will give you

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:46.519
the liberal education
that is Yale's hallmark.

00:07:46.519 --> 00:07:49.580
And by the time you graduate,
you will have furnished

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.776
your mind in the broadest possible sense,

00:07:51.776 --> 00:07:54.524
not by pursuing narrow specialized study,

00:07:54.524 --> 00:07:57.500
but instead by searching
for new knowledge,

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.770
new perspectives, new ways
of looking at the world

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.193
and by adding your own.

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:04.990
If you make good use of this place,

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:06.960
you will discover fields of knowledge

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.950
that are completely new to you,

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.890
and that you have never heard of,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.740
and you will open yourselves
to them and learn from them.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.790
To help you get as much as he can

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:18.270
out of your four years here,

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.880
you will get plenty of
advice along the way.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.230
And here are some of my
own to get you started.

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.430
Before you even start
thinking about academics

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:30.383
or world domination,
start with the basics.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.870
Take care of yourselves
and not just your own self,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.040
but the people in your lives.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:38.410
Start by listening to what
your parents and guardians

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:40.220
have been telling you all along.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:43.380
Get enough rest and sleep,
eat healthy, exercise,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.073
and spend time with your friends.

00:08:46.247 --> 00:08:49.000
In the excitement of the
discovery discovery awaiting you,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.200
it's easy to forget this good advice

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.890
so remember it in the
months and years ahead.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:56.070
And there's more beyond these basics

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:59.223
and it's just as important
if you want to flourish here.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:04.580
This past summer, the Olympic
games were held in Japan

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.690
and one of the biggest international stars

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.193
was American gymnast, Simone Biles.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.010
She is arguably the greatest
gymnast of all time.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.880
She has won 32 Olympic and
world championship medals,

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:21.650
including four gold medals
in the 2016 Olympics.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:24.856
She can perform routines of
unprecedented difficulty,

00:09:24.856 --> 00:09:26.893
and basically she can fly.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.036
With her performance at its peak

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:32.480
and expectations higher than ever,

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.330
it seemed like a foregone
conclusion that she would

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.923
take home every gold medal
in women's gymnastics.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.920
But she started off
poorly and then pulled out

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.083
for most of her events
because of the twisties,

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.630
a disability that caused
her to lose her awareness

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.910
of her balance and body position,

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:55.013
both critical to performing
complex gymnastic routines.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.740
Pushing forward risked serious injury.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.880
And so to front page headlines,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:05.223
Simone abruptly withdrew
from most of the competition.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:10.020
Her choice did prevent
her from becoming injured,

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.700
but it did more than that.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.730
It also gave her teammates
the chance to compete

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:18.340
and to gain both individual
and team medals themselves.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.700
Simply put, she showed how
taking care of yourself

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.733
can also be a way of helping others.

00:10:25.266 --> 00:10:28.830
Some people say that
winners should never quit,

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.500
that they should always grind forward,

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.643
sacrificing everything, even
their health and safety.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.630
According to this philosophy,

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:38.963
withdrawing is a sign of weakness.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.740
I disagree.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:45.150
I am a neuroscientist
and I do not distinguish

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.510
between the mind and the body.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:50.230
I see performance, brain fitness,

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.650
and mental health as interdependent.

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.453
I applauded Simone's choice.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.990
So did many professionals
who saw her withdrawal

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:02.470
as a sign of strength.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:05.830
She had the maturity and
confidence to step back,

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:07.810
to avoid injury to herself

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.410
and to avoid putting her
teammates at a disadvantage.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.240
Simone did not succumb
to outside pressures

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.360
to gain more medals.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:19.543
She did not need them to
feel valued and respected.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.380
And whether she knew it or not,

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:26.090
performance research shows
that people achieve their best

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.920
when they're not trying too hard,

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.060
but instead striking the right balance

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:34.093
of exerting themselves and
relaxing, you need both.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:36.820
And so I ask that you learned

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:39.143
from Simone's champion mindset.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:44.330
Every single one of you has
earned your spot to be at Yale,

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.233
the Olympics of higher education.

00:11:47.342 --> 00:11:51.260
You do not have to count
on any test, assignment,

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.803
or grade to prove worth.

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:58.900
What's important now is to
enjoy the process of learning

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.103
and being in community with each other.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.340
You will feel stressed at times.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.830
Yale will surely challenge you,

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.190
but it's important to pace your efforts.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:12.270
And sometimes that means stepping back,

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:15.273
taking a deep breath, and accepting help.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:19.520
And you will find people
everywhere who can provide it.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:23.930
Faculty members, heads, deans
and directors, advisors,

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.350
counselors, coaches,
chaplains, Yale health,

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.250
Yale College Community Care clinicians

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:31.850
and wellness specialists,

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.993
and your friends and family members.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:37.980
But also remember this,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.490
most of the time, others cannot remove

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:46.143
the pressures that you feel,
you need to do it for yourself.

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.070
Caring for yourself and caring
for others will help you

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:53.260
learn from each other,

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:56.133
and that is a lesson that
will enrich your time here.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.280
Yale brings together the
most interesting students

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.320
from all around the
world so that you can all

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:05.160
teach each other new
ways of understanding.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.550
You have all earned a
place in this community

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.210
because of the promise you have shown

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:10.993
in what you will contribute to it.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.556
When you get to know your
friends joining you this year,

00:13:14.556 --> 00:13:17.230
you will find that you
come from every possible

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:21.280
walk of life, bringing with
you every imaginable talent,

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:24.570
every experience, every worldview.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.240
Learn how to draw on your
different backgrounds,

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.660
views, talents, and interests,

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.963
and how to see the world
through each other's eyes.

00:13:34.543 --> 00:13:37.280
Here's a quick demonstration.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.380
It's one that shows a fundamental
principle of psychology,

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.963
of how we all experienced
the world differently.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:49.030
Your program has an inserted
side, the one with the graphic.

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:58.113
Take a look at it now, look
at the top image of the insert

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:03.530
and you'll see an ambiguous
figure that can be seen

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:05.653
in at least two different ways.

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:08.233
Name out loud what you see.

00:14:09.721 --> 00:14:12.120
(audience chattering)

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:12.953
Thank you.

00:14:14.927 --> 00:14:18.610
So some of you saw a duck,
some of you saw a rabbit.

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:20.100
And of course, if you step back,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:21.610
you can try to see it another way,

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:24.320
the other way that you
didn't see it at first.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:25.783
Can you see it both ways?

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:28.924
Okay, good.

00:14:28.924 --> 00:14:31.724
If you can't see it both ways,
we can talk to your Dean.

00:14:32.976 --> 00:14:35.730
(audience laughing)

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.090
So the way this works to help you along,

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:42.486
the duck's bill, you know,
pointing up to the top left

00:14:42.486 --> 00:14:47.280
forms the rabbit ears and the
rabbit's nose facing right,

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.233
is the back of the duck's head.

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:55.050
Now look at the cartoon
on the bottom of the page.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:57.860
There are two armies facing each other.

00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:01.630
Each army is brandishing
a flag that has the same

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.763
ambiguous duck figure,
duck/rabbit figure on it.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.640
And you'll see, on the
bottom eight general stands

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:11.785
in front of the army and
he is urging his troops

00:15:11.785 --> 00:15:14.513
with the following caption
that I will read out.

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.800
The caption for this cartoon is,

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.830
and this is again the general
speaking on the bottom,

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.550
trying to rally his troops,

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:26.447
he's saying, "There can
be no peace until they

00:15:26.447 --> 00:15:30.247
"renounce their rabbit god
and accept our duck god."

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:33.903
It's a New Yorker cartoon.

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:38.020
You know, and so the message
here is how can we help

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:41.693
everyone understand that they
are serving the same flag?

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.210
Our perceptual
interpretations, our memories

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:48.266
and our thoughts are all
constructs of the mind,

00:15:48.266 --> 00:15:50.180
and they differ across people,

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:54.980
even when considering the same
object, event, or evidence,

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:58.310
Yale will expose you to views
different from your own.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:01.325
And when it does, you can
start by finding common ground

00:16:01.325 --> 00:16:04.333
with people when you
don't agree with them.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:10.560
As Yalies, as our newest
members of Bulldog Nation,

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:13.270
you are all under one flag.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:15.960
And with the sophomores,
juniors and seniors,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.060
the alumni, faculty and
staff, and with each other,

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:22.680
you have common ground,
an immediate shared bond

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:26.240
that goes deep to the
intellectual curiosity

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:28.160
and the commitment to community

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.853
that have brought you all here.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:36.338
You have just arrived at a
place that values difference

00:16:36.338 --> 00:16:39.730
and actively seeks it out.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:43.860
It is how professors and
students find and test knowledge.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.370
And it is how the university
equips its students,

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:50.183
that's you, to become leaders
and citizens of the world.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.950
But here are the value
of seeking difference

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:57.380
is even more than that, here
it is taken as an article

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:00.280
of faith, it is the coin of the realm,

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:03.120
it is one of the bedrock
principles that governs us

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:04.833
in and out of the classroom.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:08.280
Let me assume that you have come here

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:10.020
not only to find knowledge,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:14.020
but also to learn how to
live lives of consequence.

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:16.466
If that is true, you have
come to the right place.

00:17:16.466 --> 00:17:18.506
As you take your first steps,

00:17:18.506 --> 00:17:21.810
then you will need to become
comfortable with difference,

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:25.593
not just to accept it or
tolerate it, but to insist on it,

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:30.280
you will find guides everywhere
in the classes you will take

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.000
the activities you join, and most of all,

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.670
from the people joining you right now,

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:37.060
your peers and classmates.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.510
All of them are part of
the liberal arts education

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:40.423
that awaits you.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:45.080
Your life at Yale has
started and as it does,

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:47.380
here's what I hope you will do.

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:50.040
Keep an open mind, defer judgment,

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.320
cultivate your patience, and show grace.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:56.140
If you are a duck person,
go find those rabbit people

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:58.450
and find common ground with them.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:00.471
If you are studying all duck courses,

00:18:00.471 --> 00:18:04.523
go find the rabbit courses and
see what they can teach you.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:08.620
And when you find yourself
rallying around a duck flag,

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:11.950
take a good hard look at that
rabbit flag across the field

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:14.350
and the people rallying around it,

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.653
and remember that you are all Yalies.

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.530
Members of the Class of
2025, transfer students,

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:24.830
Eli Whitney students, visiting
international students,

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:28.873
you belong to Yale and Yale
belongs to you, welcome.

00:18:30.101 --> 00:18:33.268
(audience applauding)

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:54.207
(choir singing)

00:21:23.776 --> 00:21:27.026
(audience applauding)

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:42.490
- Good morning, everyone.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:51.263
To all Eli Whitney
students, transfer students,

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:53.983
visiting international students,

00:21:55.373 --> 00:22:00.223
and first year Yale college
students, welcome to Yale.

00:22:01.549 --> 00:22:04.799
(audience applauding)

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:09.110
Let me begin by saying it is good,

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:11.723
really good to see you here today.

00:22:13.189 --> 00:22:15.130
And many families and loved ones

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:18.113
are watching today's ceremonies online.

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.660
And on behalf of my
colleagues here on stage

00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:23.623
and the entire Yale community,

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:29.600
I want to extend a warm
greeting to everyone joining us

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:32.833
wherever you are right now.

00:22:34.570 --> 00:22:39.570
This is a big moment for
you, our newest students,

00:22:39.746 --> 00:22:43.833
and for Yale, I'm so glad you're here.

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:52.070
51 years ago, university
President Kingman Brewster

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:56.680
delivered an address to the entering class

00:22:56.680 --> 00:23:01.100
of new undergraduates,
welcoming them to Yale,

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:02.763
just as I'm doing now.

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:07.090
At the time the university was coming out

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.613
of a very unusual year.

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:11.803
We can relate.

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:19.090
Just a few months earlier in
May 1970 tens of thousands

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:24.940
of people from across the
country had come to New Haven

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:29.940
and to Yale to protest
the trials of Bobby Seale

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.000
and Ericka Huggins, leaders
of the Black Panther Party

00:23:35.227 --> 00:23:38.863
who were being tried for murder.

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:44.180
Thousands of National Guard
troops had been deployed

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:49.180
to the city, as some expected
the protests to turn violent.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:54.483
The situation was extremely tense.

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:59.430
Fortunately, reason prevailed,

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:03.040
the protests remained largely peaceful

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:06.893
and no one was seriously
injured, let alone killed.

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:12.783
Still, these events rocked Yale's campus.

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:16.673
War was raging in Southeast Asia.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:20.190
Movements for civil
rights and women's rights

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:21.983
were heading in new directions.

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:27.838
And across society it seemed
like a younger generation

00:24:27.838 --> 00:24:31.913
was rising up to challenge the old guard.

00:24:33.830 --> 00:24:35.980
Against this backdrop,

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:40.173
many people were wondering
about Yale's future.

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.900
They were uncertain about
the university's role,

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:49.900
its purpose in a rapidly changing
and unpredictable society.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:58.140
Standing here today I'm feeling
many of the same emotions

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:03.073
that President Brewster
must have felt in 1970.

00:25:04.750 --> 00:25:09.073
Looking out over that
gathering of new students,

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:12.810
he knew many of them are anxious.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:17.010
He knew they had questions
about what they would do at Yale

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:22.960
and many more about the kind
of society they would encounter

00:25:23.210 --> 00:25:24.543
when they graduated.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:31.330
Yet, in his speech he
was asking them to study,

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:38.680
to go to the library, to write papers,

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:40.603
to conduct experiments.

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:45.713
He was asking them to be students.

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:51.310
And so he gave voice to a
question that was probably

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:56.310
on the minds of many, a question
I also pose to you today.

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:01.393
He asked, and these are his words,

00:26:02.495 --> 00:26:06.837
"Where then is the purpose,

00:26:06.837 --> 00:26:11.837
"which makes patient learning supportable

00:26:12.011 --> 00:26:14.813
"when the world is on fire?

00:26:16.667 --> 00:26:18.237
"Where then is the purpose,

00:26:18.237 --> 00:26:21.797
"which makes patient learning supportable

00:26:21.797 --> 00:26:23.587
"when the world is on fire?"

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:30.720
Today, again, it seems
like the world is on fire,

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:33.363
literally and metaphorically.

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:36.920
The United States is in the midst

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:39.393
of its greatest crisis since 9/11.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:42.763
We are fighting a global pandemic,

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:46.580
which will be for many of
us, the most significant

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:51.580
geopolitical and perhaps
personal event of our lives.

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:55.840
But that is not all.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.860
This summer, we have
witnessed terrible wildfires,

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:02.883
drought, and flooding in
many corners of the globe.

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:05.690
Some of you have experienced

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:08.893
these climate disasters firsthand.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:14.750
Not only climate change, but also racism,

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:19.403
extremism, the widening gulf
between the rich and poor.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:24.601
These are complex challenges
that call out for urgent

00:27:24.601 --> 00:27:26.923
and concerted action.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.557
The world is on fire, and again, we asked,

00:27:32.557 --> 00:27:34.963
what is our purpose here?

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:40.053
And how do we learn patiently,

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:44.083
seriously and rigorously,

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:48.260
as I sincerely hope you will,

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:49.893
in times such as these?

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.870
In thinking about the
answer to this question,

00:27:55.870 --> 00:28:00.870
I was reminded of Musar sometimes
called Mussar in English,

00:28:01.070 --> 00:28:06.070
a 19th century Jewish movement
that came out of Lithuania,

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:10.203
very close to where my
ancestors were rabbis.

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:14.713
The central idea of the Musar movement

00:28:14.713 --> 00:28:18.000
and of similar religious
and ethical practices

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:22.823
beyond Judaism is that
we must improve ourselves

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:28.213
before looking outward at
society seeking to change it.

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:34.600
We must examine our values,
expand our knowledge,

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:39.143
and develop our empathy and imagination.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:42.970
One of the rabbis of the time

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:44.833
is said to have told this story,

00:28:45.737 --> 00:28:50.503
"I set out to try to change
the world, but I failed.

00:28:51.407 --> 00:28:54.044
"So I decided to scale back my efforts

00:28:54.044 --> 00:28:57.963
"and only try to influence the
Jewish community of Poland.

00:28:59.017 --> 00:29:00.443
"But I failed there too.

00:29:01.647 --> 00:29:05.203
"So I targeted the community
in my hometown of Rodan,

00:29:06.297 --> 00:29:09.103
"but achieved no greater success.

00:29:10.478 --> 00:29:15.013
"Then I gave all my efforts
to changing my own family,

00:29:15.947 --> 00:29:18.363
"and failed at that as well.

00:29:19.987 --> 00:29:24.030
"Finally, I decided to change myself

00:29:25.237 --> 00:29:29.937
"and that's how I had such
an impact on the world."

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:35.140
Much like this Sage, we
are here to make an impact

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:39.513
on our communities and on the
world, don't get me wrong.

00:29:40.930 --> 00:29:44.883
But first we must start
by improving ourselves.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.130
Your college years are a time to develop

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:49.750
your strengths and talents,

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:53.860
to challenge yourself in ways
you did not think possible,

00:29:53.860 --> 00:29:58.483
to gain knowledge and
understanding, and to explore.

00:29:59.810 --> 00:30:03.590
Here at Yale, you will encounter new ideas

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:07.000
and engage with people
from different backgrounds

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:08.403
and walks of life.

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:13.770
You will take intellectual
risks and ask questions

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:17.840
about everything from the
structure of the cosmos,

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.883
to the structure of a novel.

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:27.133
Improving yourself means
leaving your comfort zone,

00:30:28.430 --> 00:30:30.130
signing up for classes

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:33.963
that sound interesting, but unfamiliar.

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:39.403
Going to office hours with
slightly intimidating professors,

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:45.390
you may be surprised by the
conversation that unfolds.

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.320
Attending talks by speakers

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:53.313
whose views are different than yours,

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:57.793
and really listening to their arguments.

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:05.350
Regardless of what you
study or the clubs you join,

00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:09.770
I promise you that you will not leave Yale

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:12.493
the same person you are today.

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:18.863
You will be changed, transformed by Yale.

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.223
We know that you are ready
for these challenges,

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:30.210
and we are excited to see what
contributions you will make

00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:33.430
to Yale, how you will write new chapters

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:38.233
in our shared history in the
coming days, weeks, and years.

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:45.963
Thinking back to that spring of 1970,

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:51.283
I'm reminded of four Yale college
students, who with others,

00:31:51.283 --> 00:31:56.133
played a pivotal role
in the mayday events.

00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:02.790
Kurt Schmoke, Ralph Dawson,
Bill Farley, Glenn de Chabert.

00:32:08.667 --> 00:32:11.597
They were serious students
and active in founding

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:18.373
and leading the Black
Student Alliance at Yale.

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:22.853
Two would be named Rhodes Scholars.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:27.940
That spring, along with Kingman Brewster,

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:30.850
his special assistant, Sam Chauncey

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:33.720
and other administrators,
these students showed

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:38.593
exemplary leadership
during a time of crisis.

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:44.840
There were instrumental in
helping keep the peace on campus

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:48.883
and most likely saving lives.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:54.470
The world was on fire,

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:56.823
but their time at Yale prepared them

00:32:56.823 --> 00:33:01.823
to tackle important challenges,
then and after graduation.

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:07.840
One as a big city mayor
and university president,

00:33:07.840 --> 00:33:10.410
others as distinguished attorneys,

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.223
all as engaged community members.

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:18.580
Like generations of alumni,

00:33:18.580 --> 00:33:20.320
these Yalies were deeply committed

00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:22.670
to making themselves better,

00:33:22.670 --> 00:33:27.670
making the university better
and making the world better.

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:35.810
You too are joining the Yale
community at a historic moment.

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:44.250
We're surrounded on all sides
by fires, small and large.

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:50.723
And yet I could think of no
better moment to be at Yale.

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:55.740
We begin this academic year
with a renewed commitment

00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:59.853
to nurture this community
and the people in it.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:06.770
Yalies, you will soon
discover love to learn,

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:12.420
they seek out new experiences
and they immerse themselves

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:16.183
fully in everything they do.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:21.080
At Yale, you can study with
top public health experts

00:34:21.080 --> 00:34:25.610
who are advising governments
on the pandemic response.

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:28.080
You can take a seminar
with a Pulitzer Prize

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:31.460
winning historian of Frederick Douglas,

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:34.833
or a leading authority on John Milton.

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:39.656
You can conduct research
alongside faculty members

00:34:39.656 --> 00:34:42.563
in over 1200 laboratories.

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:49.510
Yale's great strength now as
always is that we learn from

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:53.563
and are inspired by one another.

00:34:54.930 --> 00:34:57.530
Although we come from different places,

00:34:57.530 --> 00:35:02.530
we share a common purpose and
that is to improve ourselves

00:35:03.172 --> 00:35:06.523
so that we can improve the world.

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:11.330
Yale's mission statement
expresses our highest ambitions.

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:12.820
It says in part,

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:17.276
Yale is committed to
improving the world today

00:35:17.276 --> 00:35:20.363
and for future generations.

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:27.380
It goes on to say, Yale educates
aspiring leaders worldwide

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:31.293
who serve all sectors of society.

00:35:33.140 --> 00:35:37.883
You, you are those aspiring leaders.

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:43.310
And this mission is our
answer to the question

00:35:43.310 --> 00:35:46.773
my predecessor asked over 50 years ago.

00:35:48.550 --> 00:35:53.550
I believe the patient learning
President Brewster spoke of

00:35:53.730 --> 00:35:57.560
means deep engagement in your studies.

00:35:57.560 --> 00:36:01.550
It means challenging your
thoughts and beliefs.

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:05.710
It means expanding the
frontiers of knowledge,

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:09.253
your own, and then the worlds.

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:15.270
It means using your
time at Yale to prepare

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:16.553
for the trials ahead.

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:23.670
In this sense patient learning
is not only supportable,

00:36:24.286 --> 00:36:29.100
but essential if we are
to fulfill Yale's mission,

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:32.203
and indeed improve the world.

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:37.510
I will end with lyrics
written by one of my favorite

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:42.510
folk singers, Woody Guthrie
in his song Worlds on Fire.

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:47.183
His words are only too applicable.

00:36:48.733 --> 00:36:53.733
While the skies, they're
clearing we'll rise up dreaming.

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:59.303
Build our city from the ashes.

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:05.590
Yes, the world is on fire,
but right in front of me,

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.873
I see many reasons for optimism.

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:17.260
Together, as part of this
community, you will dream.

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:21.303
You will build.

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:27.290
And you will prepare for lives
of leadership and service.

00:37:32.300 --> 00:37:34.203
Welcome to Yale.

00:37:35.526 --> 00:37:38.693
(audience applauding)

00:37:56.607 --> 00:37:59.274
(choir singing)

00:40:51.389 --> 00:40:54.639
(audience applauding)

00:41:01.082 --> 00:41:02.332
- Please stand.

00:41:10.450 --> 00:41:12.623
My beloved Yale family,

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:18.010
as we ready ourselves to
step out onto new ground

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:22.340
in this daunting yet
inspiring liminal space

00:41:22.340 --> 00:41:27.340
of the unknown next, we begin
a poignant turn of goodbyes

00:41:27.970 --> 00:41:32.970
and hellos that invite laughter,
tears, challenge, choice,

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:36.783
discovery, and pride.

00:41:37.760 --> 00:41:42.540
Let us go forth with deep
trust in certain knowledge

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:46.913
that in this place each and
every one of us is welcome,

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:52.303
that in this place, we
will both stumble and soar,

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:57.740
that in this place love surrounds us,

00:41:57.740 --> 00:42:00.830
beauty can surprise us.

00:42:00.830 --> 00:42:04.793
In this place, light and truth awaits us.

00:42:05.670 --> 00:42:10.020
On this day and every day
forward may all that is holy

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:15.020
shine upon us and tenderly
bless our every breath.

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:20.870
May we go in Shalom, in Salaam, in Shanti,

00:42:21.730 --> 00:42:23.803
may we go in peace,

00:42:24.710 --> 00:42:25.883
Let us begin.

00:42:34.729 --> 00:42:38.146
(upbeat classical music)

